<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">George Gershwin</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1898">1898</a>–<a href="/years/1937">1937</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/20th-century">20th century</a> · Also known as Jacob Gershwine</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">George Gershwin was an American composer and pianist whose works spanned jazz, popular and classical music. Born in Brooklyn to Jewish immigrants from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russian-empire">Russian Empire</a>, he began as a song plugger and wrote Broadway <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> works with his brother Ira Gershwin. His works include Rhapsody in Blue (1924), An American in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a> (1928), the songs Swanee and I Got Rhythm, and the opera Porgy and Bess (1935), written with Ira and DuBose Heyward, which was at first a commercial failure. His Of Thee I Sing (1931) was the first musical to win the Pulitzer Prize for Drama. He moved to Hollywood to compose film scores and died of a brain tumor in 1937, aged 38.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/george-gershwin.jpg" alt="George Gershwin" width="330" height="423" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Carl Van Vechten · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Portrait_of_George_Gershwin_LCCN2004662906.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Gershwin was born on 26 September 1898 in an apartment on Snediker Avenue in the East New York neighbourhood of Brooklyn. His birth certificate names him Jacob Gershwine, and the surname was pronounced Gershvin in the Yiddish-speaking community. His father Moishe, who took the name Morris, had arrived in New York on 14 August 1890 to escape compulsory military service in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a>. His mother Roza, who became Rose, was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-petersburg">Saint Petersburg</a>.</p><p>The family moved often and lived mostly in the Yiddish Theater District, where George and Ira frequented the local <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatres</a> and George occasionally appeared on stage as an extra. In 1913, aged 15, he left school to work as a song plugger on Tin Pan Alley for $15 a week.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p>From 1916 Gershwin recorded and arranged piano rolls, in all more than a hundred and forty, under his own name and pseudonyms, a main source of income until his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> earnings made them superfluous. His first song on Broadway, Making of a Girl, was written in 1916 with Sigmund Romberg. In 1924 he composed Rhapsody in Blue for orchestra and piano, orchestrated by Ferde Grofé and premiered by Paul Whiteman's band, followed in 1925 by the Piano Concerto in F. With Ira he wrote Lady Be Good in 1924 and Girl Crazy in 1930, which introduced Embraceable You and I Got Rhythm.</p><p>When he asked to study in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, Nadia Boulanger refused him, fearing rigorous training would spoil his jazz-influenced style, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maurice-ravel">Maurice Ravel</a> voiced similar objections. In the summer of 1934 he stayed on Folly Island, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-carolina">South Carolina</a>, at DuBose Heyward's invitation, and there found the inspiration for Porgy and Bess. After its commercial failure he moved to Hollywood and wrote the score of Shall We Dance in 1936.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>In early 1937 Gershwin began to suffer blinding headaches and a recurring impression of a smell of burning <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rubber">rubber</a>. On 11 February 1937 he played his Piano Concerto in F with the San Francisco Symphony under Pierre Monteux and suffered coordination problems and blackouts.</p><p>He died on 11 July 1937 of a brain tumour, without a will and his estate passed to his mother. His only Academy Award nomination, for They Can't Take That Away from Me, was posthumous. George and Ira received the Congressional Gold Medal in 1985, a special Pulitzer Prize was awarded to George in 1998 for the centennial of his birth, and the Library of Congress created its Gershwin Prize for Popular Song in 2007.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “George Gershwin” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Shuffle Along proved that Black and white audiences would pay to see African Americans on Broadway; all-black productions of <b>George Gershwin</b>’s Porgy and Bess and of Four Saints in Three Acts followed, and composers carried blues, spirituals and jazz into the concert hall.</blockquote><a href="/movement/harlem-renaissance">Harlem Renaissance · Outcomes</a></li><li><blockquote><b>George Gershwin</b> borrowed Handy's themes for his own music, including Rhapsody in Blue, and sent Handy a signed copy of the score acknowledging that his early blues were &quot;the forefathers of this work&quot;.</blockquote><a href="/songs/saint-louis-blues">Saint Louis Blues</a></li></ul></section>
